<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Corsica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Corsica is a mountainous island in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. It lay within the sphere of Carthaginian influence before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> seized it, together with Sardinia, in 238–237 BCE during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary-war">Mercenary War</a>. The seizure, in violation of the peace treaty, deepened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-empire">Punic Empire</a>'s resentment of Rome.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/corsica.jpg" alt="Corsica" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Corsica_in_France_2016.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Corsica is the fourth-largest island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, lying south-east of the French mainland, west of the Italian Peninsula and immediately north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. It is 183 km long at its longest and 83 km wide at its widest. It is the most mountainous island in the Mediterranean, a mountain in the sea: a single chain makes up two-thirds of the island, with Monte Cinto the highest peak at 2,706 metres. Its granite backbone on the western side formed about 250 million years ago.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People have lived on Corsica since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a>, and a permanent presence is documented from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. After the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> collapsed in the fifth century CE the island was invaded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, briefly recovered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, then became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Kingdom of the Lombards</a>. Pepin the Short, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, expelled the Lombards and nominally granted Corsica to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-stephen-ii">Pope Stephen II</a>.</p><p>In the first quarter of the 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> together freed the island from the threat of Arab invasion, and it then came under the influence of the Republic of Pisa. Immigration from Tuscany gave the island its place names and made the language of the northern two-thirds close to Tuscan. The Republic of Genoa ruled Corsica from 1284 to 1755.</p><p>The Corsican Revolution against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> began in 1729 under Luiggi Giafferi and Giacinto Paoli, later led by Giacinto's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a>. An independent Corsican Republic was proclaimed in 1755 and remained sovereign until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> conquered the island in 1769. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> was born in Ajaccio in 1769. In 1814 British troops briefly occupied the island and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bastia">Bastia</a> gave the British crown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, but Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castlereagh">Castlereagh</a> later repudiated it. Corsica was freed in 1943, the first French department to be liberated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Corsica is one of the 18 regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and a single territorial collectivity, with a Corsican Assembly that has more extensive powers than other French regional bodies. Ajaccio is the capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bastia">Bastia</a> the second largest town. The population is 365,636. French is the official and most widely spoken language, while Corsican, an Italo-Dalmatian language close to medieval Tuscan, is recognised as a regional language. The Scandola Nature Reserve, within the regional natural park, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Corsica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Competition for sea routes brought the cities into alliance with Carthage against the Phocaean Greeks, who in the 6th century BCE were founding colonies on the coasts of Sardinia, Spain and <b>Corsica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Corsica</b>, held by Genoa, was never conquered, and the claim to it remained a title only.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Between 1346 and 1355 the Grimaldi annexed Menton and Roquebrune, increasing their territory almost tenfold, and in the late 1300s Genoa lost Monaco while fighting the Crown of Aragon over <b>Corsica</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome opportunistically seized Sardinia and <b>Corsica</b> in 238–237 BCE during the Mercenary War, in breach of the spirit of the peace, and added a further indemnity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bank of Saint George, a creditors’ bank founded in 1407, governed the island of <b>Corsica</b> on the republic’s behalf for over a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gaiseric conquered Sicily, Sardinia, <b>Corsica</b>, Malta and the Balearic Islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
